// Generated by tools/build_content.py. Part 6: Denormalization
export default {"lead": "<p>Denormalization means deliberately choosing a lower normal form. You accept redundancy, and the anomalies it can cause, in exchange for faster queries.</p>", "secs": [{"id": "p6-definitions", "t": "Definitions", "h": "<ul><li><strong>Denormalization</strong> (lecture): taking normalized relations and joining them to produce a relation in a lower normal form.</li><li><strong>Denormalization</strong> (textbook): the reverse of normalization; consciously choosing a lower normal form.</li><li>CUST_ORDER in Part 5 is a denormalized relation: it is CUSTOMERS joined to ORDERS.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p6-why-anyone-would-do-it", "t": "Why anyone would do it", "h": "<ul><li>Every step up the normal forms splits relations, so a highly normalized database has many relations.</li><li>Getting useful answers back out means joining those relations together again.</li><li>Joins are expensive in processing time and throughput, and the cost grows with the size of the relations.</li><li>Fewer relations means fewer joins, so lower normal forms can be queried more efficiently.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p6-the-trade-off", "t": "The trade-off", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Highly normalized (3NF, BCNF)</th><th>Denormalized (1NF, 2NF)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Redundancy</td><td>Minimal</td><td>Deliberately repeated data</td></tr><tr><td>Modification anomalies</td><td>Prevented by the structure</td><td>Possible; must be managed some other way</td></tr><tr><td>Number of relations</td><td>Many</td><td>Few</td></tr><tr><td>Joins needed per query</td><td>Many</td><td>Few</td></tr><tr><td>Fast at</td><td>Writing: inserts, updates, deletes</td><td>Reading: queries and analysis</td></tr><tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Operational, transaction processing (OLTP)</td><td>Decision-making, analytical (OLAP), data warehouses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "p6-where-denormalization-is-appropriate", "t": "Where denormalization is appropriate", "h": "<ul><li><strong>Decision-making databases.</strong> Once entered, the data is generally not modified, so modification anomalies are unlikely. These are often run as read-only databases for that reason. With a very large number of records, a small factual error is unlikely to change a decision based on the whole data set.</li><li><strong>Data warehouses.</strong> The traditional star schema is only in 1NF.</li><li><strong>Archiving.</strong> Data is often denormalized when it is moved from a transactional database into a read-only decision-making database.</li><li><strong>A few operational systems of massive size</strong>, where joining very large relations is too slow. Anomalies must then be handled by techniques other than normalization.</li><li><strong>Temporary tables</strong> that will soon be discarded. This is also where flattening, the weakest way to reach 1NF, can be a reasonable choice.</li></ul><p>Where it is not appropriate: databases where errors are costly in dollars, customer service or competitive position. If NSFdb mishandles an order or shipment, a customer gets the wrong product or none at all, so it must stay highly normalized.</p>"}, {"id": "p6-when-to-stop-normalizing", "t": "When to stop normalizing", "h": "<ul><li>When applications would need too many joins.</li><li>When no decomposition preserves the dependencies you need. This is the usual reason to stop at 3NF and not go on to BCNF.</li></ul><p>Textbook example. <code class=\"rel\">Emp(<span class=\"pk\">empId</span>, lastName, firstName, street, city, state, zip)</code> has the transitive dependency zip → city, state, so it is not in 3NF. Normalizing gives <code class=\"rel\">Emp1(<span class=\"pk\">empId</span>, name, street, <span class=\"fk\">zip</span>)</code> and <code class=\"rel\">Codes(<span class=\"pk\">zip</span>, city, state)</code>, but then every request for a full address needs a join. A designer may reasonably leave Emp as it is in 2NF.</p>"}, {"id": "p6-two-rules-to-remember", "t": "Two rules to remember", "h": "<ul><li>A database's normal form is the <strong>lowest</strong> normal form found in any of its relations.</li><li>Relations in one database do not all have to be in the same normal form. The decision can be made relation by relation.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p6-non-normalized-databases", "t": "Non-normalized databases", "h": "<p>The textbook goes a step further: some systems are not built on normalized relations at all.</p><ul><li>Normalized relational databases suit simple, stable, current data and are the standard for OLTP. They are optimized for writing; reads can be slow because of joins.</li><li>OLAP systems need historical data and rare updates, so they are optimized for reading, and data may be repeated to avoid joins.</li><li>Technologies that store denormalized data include XML, Google's BigTable, HBase, Cassandra and Hadoop. Object-based databases are by nature not normalized.</li></ul>"}]};
